      title: "The Red Scarf in the Hallway",
      image: "",
      imageAlt: "",
      text: `The following original literary excerpt presents a moment of withheld emotion and shifting perception.\n\nMara waited on the back step until the kitchen clock struck eight, though no one in the house had asked her to wait. The rain had stopped, leaving the yard bright with puddles that held pieces of the white sky. When Jonas opened the door, Mara noticed that he carried the blue cup instead of the brown one, a change so small that it seemed almost rude to mention it.\n\nInside, the table was set for three. The empty chair faced the window, where the pear tree leaned close enough to scrape the glass. Jonas spoke about the weather, the price of coal, and the neighbor's new fence, but each subject arrived too quickly and left too soon. Mara answered with the careful politeness of someone stepping across a frozen pond, listening for the first crack beneath her own voice.\n\nAt last, Jonas placed the blue cup before the empty chair. No one looked at it directly. Yet the cup seemed to gather the whole room around itself, making the silence heavier than accusation and more difficult to refuse.`,
